data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider already threw `InvalidOperationException` on synchronous LINQ operations by default, but you could opt back in with:

```csharp
options.UseCosmos(...).AllowSynchronousIO();
```

**In .NET 11, that opt-in is removed entirely.** All four of your sync calls — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, and `SaveChanges()` — will fail at runtime with `InvalidOperationException` regardless of configuration.

## The fix: go fully async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()`
- `First()` → `FirstAsync()`
- `Count()` → `CountAsync()`
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()`
- All methods become `async Task<T>` and callers must be updated to `await`

You'll also need to propagate `async/await` up through your call chain (controllers, services, etc.) and remove the `AllowSynchronousIO()` configuration since it no longer compiles.
